The factors that affect the coordination geometry of the iron (III) ion in the iron (III) tetraphenylporphyrin complex include:

1. The size and size of the ligands: The size of the ligands can affect the coordination geometry by influencing the distance between the metal ion and the ligands. Larger ligands may result in a more distorted octahedral geometry, while smaller ligands may result in a more regular octahedral geometry.

2. The charge of the ligands: The charge of the ligands can also affect the coordination geometry by influencing the strength of the metal-ligand bond. Ligands with higher charges will form stronger bonds with the metal ion, which can lead to a more stable coordination geometry.

