","When solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) reacts with sodium hydroxide (NaOH), the observable effects are:
1. The solid benzoic acid dissolves in the solution.
2. The solution becomes basic (pH > 7) due to the formation of the conjugate base (benzoate ion, C₇H₅O₂⁻).
3. The solution may also become slightly oily due to the formation of benzoate ion, which is soluble in water but less soluble than benzoic acid.

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:
\[
\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 + \text{NaOH} \rightarrow \text{C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{Na}^+
\]
This equation shows the complete ionization of benzoic acid and sodium hydroxide, forming the benzoate ion (C₇H₅O₂⁻), water (H₂O), and sodium ion (Na⁺).
