","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: structural isomerism and optical isomerism.

1. Structural isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when the molecules have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms in their structure. In the case of C4H10O, there are two types of structural isomerism possible:

   a. Chain isomerism: This occurs when the carbon chain is different in the isomers. For example, butanol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) and 2-methylpropanol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3) are chain isomers.

   b. Functional isomerism: This occurs when the isomers have different functional groups. For example, C4H10O can form butan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) and butan-2-ol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3), which are functional isomers.

2. Optical isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when a molecule has a chiral center, which means it has four different groups attached to a carbon atom. In C4H10O, the molecule 2-butanol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3) has a chiral center at the second carbon atom, and thus exhibits optical isomerism. The two optical isomers are called enantiomers, and they are non-superimposable mirror images of each other.

In summary, the possible types of isomerism for C4H10O are structural isomerism (chain and functional isomerism) and optical isomerism.
