","To calculate the amount of heat released when 2.5 moles of propane are burned, we can use the given standard enthalpy of combustion and the stoichiometry of the balanced equation.

The standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is -2220 kJ/mol, which means that 1 mole of propane releases 2220 kJ of heat when burned.

To find the heat released by 2.5 moles of propane, we can use the following equation:

Heat released = (moles of propane) × (standard enthalpy of combustion per mole)

Heat released = (2.5 moles) × (-2220 kJ/mol)

Heat released = -5550 kJ

The negative sign indicates that the heat is released (exothermic reaction).

So, the amount of heat released when 2.5 moles of propane is completely burned in oxygen gas is 5550 kJ.
