","Isotopologues and isotopomers are two terms used in chemistry to describe molecules with different isotopic compositions. The key difference lies in whether the isotopic substitution affects the molecular structure or only the mass distribution.

1. **Isotopologues**: These are molecules that have the same molecular structure but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_2 \) (acetone) can have different isotopologues: \( \text{C}_6^{12}\text{H}_2^{16}\text{O}_2 \), \( \text{C}_6^{13}\text{H}_2^{16}\text{O}_2 \), or \( \text{C}_6^{12}\text{H}_2^{18}\text{O}_2 \). The only difference is the isotopic composition of the carbon or oxygen atoms, but the connectivity of the atoms remains the same.

2. **Isotomers**: These are molecules that have the same molecular formula but different connectivity of atoms due to isotopic substitution. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_4 \) (ethene) and \( \text{C}_2\text{D}_4 \) (dethane) are isotomers because they have the same molecular formula but the carbon-hydrogen bonds are replaced by carbon-deogen bonds.

In summary, isotopologues are molecules with the same structure but different isotopic compositions, while isotomers are molecules with the same molecular formula but different connectivity of atoms due to isotopic substitution.
