\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]

This equation is derived from the definition of the derivative as the derivative \( f'(a) \) is the slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \). The equation is in the form of the firstformula for the tangent line equation:

\[
y = mx + c
\]

where \( m = f'(a) \) and \( c = f(a) - f'(a)a \). 

Thus, the equation \( y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a) \) is the equation of the tangent line to the graph of \( f \) at \( (a, f(a)) \). 

\[
\boxed{y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)}
\]
