<USER_REQUEST>
Write a recurrence relation for the number of comparisons between list elements in QuickSort, assuming each pivot element splits its sublist into two equal-length lists (best-case scenario). Explain the reasoning behind the recurrence relation.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in QuickSort in the best-case scenario is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C(n/2) + C(n/2) + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
This recurrence relation is derived as follows:
1. **Base Case (n = 1):** If the input list has only one element, there are no comparisons needed, so \( C(1) = 0 \).
2. **General Case (n > 1):**
   - The first step of QuickSearch is to choose a pivot event.
   - The pivot divides the input list into two equal-sized sublists, each of size \( n/2 \).
   - The number of comparisons required to process each subleaf is \( C(n/2) \).
   - The first step also requires \( n - 1 \) comparisons to compare the pivot element with all other elements in the input line.
   - Thus, the total number of comparisons is the sum of the counts for the two subleaves plus the \( n - 1 \) comparisons for the pivot.
   - Thus, \( C(n) = C(n/2) + C(n/2) + n - 1 \).

This recurrence relation is derived from the standard QuickSearch workflow and the assumption that the pivot divides the input list into two equal-sized subleaves.

