a walks at 10 kmph and 3 hours after his start , b cycles after him at 20 kmph . how far from the start does b catch up with a ?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Calculate the distance A travels before B starts:**  
   \[
   \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ h} = 30 \text{ km}
   \]  
   A travels 30 km before B starts.

2. **Determine the relative speed of B compared to A:**  
   \[
   \text{Relative speed} = \text{Speed of B} - \text{Speed of A} = 20 \text{ kmph} - 10 \text{ kmph} = 10 \text{ kmph}
   \]  
   B moves 10 kmph faster than A, so the relative speed is 10 kmph.

3. **Calculate the time taken for B to cover the difference in distance:**  
   \[
   \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}} = \frac{30 \text{ km}}{10 \text{ kmph}} = 3 \text{ h}
   \]  
   B covers the 30 km difference in 3 hours.

4. **Determine the total distance B travels when catching A:**  
   \[
   \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 20 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ h} = 60 \text{ km}
   \]  
   B covers 60 km when catching A.

5. **Conclusion:**  
   B catches A at a distance of \(\boxed{60 \text{ km}}\) from the start.
