## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
What is the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron is calculated using the Compton effect formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda' - \lambda = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\lambda'\) is the scattered wavelength,
- \(\lambda\) is the initial wavelength,
- \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.

The maximum wavelength change occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\), so \(\cos \theta = -1\). Substituting \(\lambda = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}\):

\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \pi \times 1.054 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 2.998 \times 10^8} (1 + 1) = 4.84 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}
\]

The percentage change is:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{4.84 \times 10^{-11}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \right) \times 100 = 24.2\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{24.2\%}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

