</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The work done on the gas is given by the integral of the pressure \( P \) with respect to volume \( V \):
\[
W = \int_{V_1}^{V_2} P \, dV
\]
For an isothermal process, the pressure \( P \) is related to the volume \( V \) by the ideal gas law:
\[
P = \frac{RT}{V}
\]
Substituting this into the integral:
\[
W = \int_{V_1}^{V_2} \frac{RT}{V} \, dV = RT \int_{V_1}^{V_2} \frac{dV}{V}
\]
Evaluating the integral:
\[
W = RT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right)
\]
Given that the volume is increased by a factor of \( n \), \( V_2 = n V_1 \). Substituting this:
\[
W = RT \ln \left( \frac{n V_1}{V_1} \right) = RT \ln n
\]
Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{RT \ln n}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

