","The net chemical reaction during the discharging of a lead storage battery is:
\[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 4\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

**Explanation:**
1. **Oxidation at the anode (anode reaction):**
   \[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4(aq) \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + \text{H}^+(aq) \]
   - Lead (Pb) is oxidized to lead sulfate (PbSO₄).

2. **Reduction at the cathode (cathode reaction):**
   \[ \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 4\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]
   - Lead dioxide (PbO₂) is reduced to lead sulfate (PbSO₄).

3. **Net reaction:**
