A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The force on the proton is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength. The acceleration \(a\) is then given by \(a = \frac{F}{m}\), where \(m\) is the mass of the proton.  
\[
F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.85 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.85 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial speed \(u = 0 \, \text{m/s}\). The final speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\).  
The time \(t\) taken by the proton to reach this speed is given by \(v = u + at\).  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.85 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 6.31 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}
\]  
Thus, the time taken is \(\boxed{6.31 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}}\).
