In a Cartesian coordinate system, what is the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\)? Express the result in terms of its components.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system is given by the divergence operator \(\nabla \times \mathbf{V}\). In Cartesian coordinates, this is given by:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \nabla \times (V_1 \mathbf{i} + V_2 \mathbf{j}) = \left( \frac{\partial V_2}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_1}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

Since \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), the partial derivatives with respect to \(z\) are zero. Thus, the curl simplifies to:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_2}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_1}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is a vector with components:

\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_2}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_1}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

where \(\mathbf{k}\) is the unit vector along the \(z\)-axis.
