\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \geq \frac{h}{2}
\]  
Here, \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}\) J·s.  

1. **Calculate the uncertainty in energy (\(\Delta E\)):**  
   \[
   \Delta E \geq \frac{h}{2 \Delta t} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J·s}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 1.814 \times 10^{-11} \text{ J}
   \]  
   Convert this to MeV:  
   \[
   \Delta E = 1.814 \times 10^{-11} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} = 11.4 \text{ MeV}
   \]  

2. **Determine the range of outcomes:**  
   The rest energy of the \(\Sigma^+\) is 1385 MeV, and the uncertainty is 11.4 MeV. The range of outcomes is:  
   \[
   1385 \text{ MeV} - 11.4 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1385 \text{ MeV} + 11.4 \text{ MeV}
   \]  
   \[
   \boxed{1373.6 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1396.4 \text{ MeV}}
   \]  

Thus, the typical range of outcomes is \(\boxed{1373.6 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1396.4 \text{ MeV}}\).
